.TH CYAML_LIST_PUSH 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_list_push \- append a child to a list
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

ccol_retval_t cyaml_list_push(cyaml seq, cyaml child);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Appends child to the CYAML_LIST node seq.  On success seq owns child,
so don't free child after such a call.

Each return code carries one rule about child.  ccol_invalid_args
always means that the function refused the arguments and didn't touch
child at all, so the caller owns child and must destroy it itself.
Every other failure takes child and deep-frees it before the function
returns, so the caller must not destroy child after any of them.
So the return code alone tells the caller what to do with child; a code
that meant different things on different paths couldn't do that.

These give ccol_invalid_args: a NULL child, a NULL seq, a seq that is
not a CYAML_LIST, a child that is attached to a list or dictionary
parent, and a child that is seq itself.  An attached child would end
up with two owners if the function took it, and each owner frees the
node when its own parent is destroyed.  A node you just created is
always fine, and so is a fresh cyaml_clone().  A borrowed reference
from cyaml_get(), cyaml_list_get() or cyaml_dictionary_get() never
is, because each of those names a node that has a parent.

child must not hold seq anywhere in its own subtree either, and the
function doesn't check this.  A cycle made that way turns a later
cyaml_destroy(), cyaml_clone() or cyaml_serialize() call on the tree
into undefined behaviour.  The attachment rule above already excludes
the case for every node that has a parent, so the only ancestor left
unchecked is the one with no parent: the root of the tree that seq
belongs to.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
seq    The target node, a CYAML_LIST.
child  The node to append.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success, ccol_invalid_args, ccol_not_enough_memory, or
ccol_container_full.
ccol_invalid_args leaves child untouched and with the caller.  It is
returned when seq or child is NULL, when seq is not a list, when child
is attached, or when child is seq itself.
ccol_container_full means that the list holds the largest element
count it can represent, which a real program doesn't reach.
On every outcome other than ccol_invalid_args the call takes child.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_list_get (3),
.BR cyaml_list_len (3),
.BR cyaml_create_list (3)
